Below is a quick‑start guide you can drop into your project.  
It shows exactly how to feed your **`myItemList`** into the helper routine so that:

1. All *“urgent”* items bubble up to the very front of the result set.  
2. Everything else stays alphabetical – and because you asked for “ascending”, it will be in normal A→Z order.

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Your original unsorted array
const myItemList = [
    { name: 'Banana',   status: 'normal' },
    { name: 'Apple',    status: 'urgent' },
    { name: 'Cherry',   status: 'normal' },
    { name: 'Date',     status: 'urgent' },
    { name: 'Elderberry', status: 'normal' }
];
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Helper that does two things:
 *
 * • Pushes all objects whose `status === priorityStatus`
 *   to the beginning of the returned array.
 * • Sorts the remaining objects by their `name` field.
 *
 * @param {Array<Object>} items          - Array of objects to sort.
 * @param {string}        priorityStatus - Status value that gets precedence.
 * @param {boolean}       ascending      - true → A–Z, false → Z–A for non‑priority items.
 */
function prioritizeAndSort({ items, priorityStatus, ascending }) {
    const [priorityItems, otherItems] = items.reduce(
        ([prio, others], item) =>
            item.status === priorityStatus ? [[...prio, item], others]
                                            : [prio, [...others, item]],
        [[], []]
    );

    otherItems.sort((a, b) => {
        if (a.name < b.name) return ascending ? -1 : 1;
        if (a.name > b.name) return ascending ? 1 : -1;
        return 0;
    });

    return [...priorityItems, ...otherItems];
}
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
    items:         myItemList,
    priorityStatus:'urgent',
    ascending:     true           // change to false for reverse order
});

console.log(sorted);
/*
[
  { name: 'Apple',    status: 'urgent' },   // urgent first
  { name: 'Date',     status: 'urgent' },

  { name: 'Banana',   status: 'normal' },   // rest sorted A-Z
  { name: 'Cherry',   status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Elderberry', status: 'normal' }
]
*/
```

### How it works under the hood

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Partition** | Split the incoming array into two buckets:<br>• `priorityItems`: those where `item.status === priorityStatus`.<br>• `otherItems`: everything else. |
| **Sort** | Run a standard `.sort()` on `otherItems`, comparing the `name` strings. Pass `ascending` to decide direction (`<=>`). |
| **Concatenate** | Return `[…priorityItems, …sortedOtherItems]`; this guarantees all urgent entries come before anything else. |

---

#### If you want to run this directly in Node.js / browser console

Just copy/paste the whole block above. No external dependencies required!  

If you already have a library that exposes the same logic, replace the body of `prioritizeAndSort` with whatever implementation you prefer—just keep the signature identical.

Happy coding!
